1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,605
In this lesson, we'll measure angles a new
way, in radians.

2
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:10,270
An angle in standard position starts on
the positive x axis and rotates.

3
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:14,269
Counterclockwise is positive, clockwise is
negative.

4
00:00:14,719 --> 00:00:19,683
Angles that end in the same place, like
thirty degrees and three hundred ninety

5
00:00:19,683 --> 00:00:21,631
degrees, are called coterminal.

6
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:27,204
One radian is the angle that cuts off an
arc as long as the radius.

7
00:00:27,654 --> 00:00:33,666
A full circle is two pi radians, so three
hundred sixty degrees equals two pi, and

8
00:00:33,666 --> 00:00:36,379
one hundred eighty degrees equals pi.

9
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:42,471
To change degrees to radians, multiply by
pi over one hundred eighty.

10
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:49,096
Sixty degrees times pi over one eighty is
pi over three. One hundred thirty five

11
00:00:49,096 --> 00:00:51,411
degrees is three pi over four.

12
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:56,011
To go back, multiply by one hundred eighty
over pi.

13
00:00:56,461 --> 00:01:01,709
Five pi over six times one eighty over pi
is one hundred fifty degrees.

14
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:08,337
Radians make arc length simple: s equals r
theta, with theta in radians.

15
00:01:08,787 --> 00:01:15,117
On a circle of radius six, an angle of pi
over three cuts off an arc of six times pi

16
00:01:15,117 --> 00:01:19,262
over three, which is two pi, about six
point two eight.

17
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,304
Your turn. Convert two hundred twenty five
degrees to radians.

18
00:01:24,754 --> 00:01:26,290
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:31,369
Two twenty five times pi over one eighty
is five pi over four.

20
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,681
In this lesson, we'll meet the unit
circle, the key to trigonometry.

21
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:43,825
The unit circle has radius one, centered
at the origin. Rotate by an angle theta

22
00:01:43,825 --> 00:01:45,331
and land on a point.

23
00:01:45,781 --> 00:01:51,541
That point's x coordinate is cosine theta,
and its y coordinate is sine theta.

24
00:01:52,667 --> 00:01:58,493
At pi over six, the point is root three
over two, one half. At pi over three, it's

25
00:01:58,493 --> 00:02:00,624
one half, root three over two.

26
00:02:01,074 --> 00:02:03,975
These come from the thirty sixty ninety
triangle.

27
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:08,705
At pi over four, both coordinates are root
two over two.

28
00:02:09,155 --> 00:02:12,803
The triangle is isosceles, so x and y are
equal.

29
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:17,901
In other quadrants, reuse the reference
angle and fix the signs.

30
00:02:18,351 --> 00:02:24,361
Five pi over six has reference angle pi
over six, but it's in quadrant two, where

31
00:02:24,361 --> 00:02:30,149
x is negative. So cosine is negative root
three over two and sine is one half.

32
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:34,765
Tangent is sine over cosine, the slope of
the radius.

33
00:02:35,215 --> 00:02:41,742
Tangent of pi over four is one. Tangent of
pi over two is undefined, since cosine is

34
00:02:41,742 --> 00:02:42,597
zero there.

35
00:02:43,733 --> 00:02:47,424
Your turn. Find cosine and sine of four pi
over three.

36
00:02:47,874 --> 00:02:49,410
Pause the video and try it.

37
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:55,821
Reference angle pi over three in quadrant
three, so cosine is negative one half and

38
00:02:55,821 --> 00:02:58,479
sine is negative root three over two.

39
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,757
In this lesson, we'll turn the unit circle
into waves.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,715
As the angle goes around the circle, the
height of the point rises and falls.

41
00:03:09,165 --> 00:03:12,194
Plot that height against the angle, and
you get a wave.

42
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:19,786
y equals sine x starts at zero, peaks at
one at pi over two, returns to zero at pi,

43
00:03:19,786 --> 00:03:26,005
bottoms out at negative one at three pi
over two, and is back to zero at two pi.

44
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:27,565
Then it repeats.

45
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:33,308
y equals cosine x has the same shape, but
starts at its peak, one.

46
00:03:33,758 --> 00:03:37,129
It's just the sine wave shifted left by pi
over two.

47
00:03:38,267 --> 00:03:42,363
Both waves have period two pi, the length
of one full cycle.

48
00:03:42,813 --> 00:03:48,584
Their amplitude is one: half the distance
from the lowest to the highest point. The

49
00:03:48,584 --> 00:03:50,322
midline is y equals zero.

50
00:03:51,467 --> 00:03:57,432
Use the pattern to read values. Where is
sine x equal to one between zero and two

51
00:03:57,432 --> 00:03:57,653
pi?

52
00:03:58,103 --> 00:04:03,415
Only at pi over two. And cosine x equals
negative one only at pi.

53
00:04:04,533 --> 00:04:09,397
Your turn. Between zero and two pi, where
does cosine x equal zero?

54
00:04:09,847 --> 00:04:11,383
Pause the video and try it.

55
00:04:11,833 --> 00:04:14,287
At pi over two and three pi over two.

56
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:20,328
In this lesson, we'll transform sine and
cosine graphs, just like any other

57
00:04:20,328 --> 00:04:20,916
function.

58
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:26,321
In y equals a sine x, the number a
stretches the wave vertically.

59
00:04:26,771 --> 00:04:32,749
y equals three sine x swings from negative
three to three. Its amplitude is the

60
00:04:32,749 --> 00:04:34,792
absolute value of a, three.

61
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:40,456
In y equals sine of b x, the period
becomes two pi over b.

62
00:04:40,906 --> 00:04:45,471
y equals sine two x completes a cycle in
pi, twice as fast.

63
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:53,277
Adding d moves the midline. y equals sine
x plus two oscillates around y equals two.

64
00:04:53,727 --> 00:04:55,412
It goes from one up to three.

65
00:04:56,533 --> 00:05:00,650
Replacing x with x minus c shifts the wave
right by c.

66
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:07,295
y equals sine of x minus pi over two is
the cosine wave flipped, it starts at its

67
00:05:07,295 --> 00:05:08,290
lowest point.

68
00:05:08,433 --> 00:05:16,004
The tide height is h equals four cosine of
pi over six t, plus ten, with t in hours.

69
00:05:16,454 --> 00:05:22,816
Amplitude four, midline ten feet, period
two pi over pi over six, which is twelve

70
00:05:22,816 --> 00:05:26,822
hours. High tide is fourteen feet, low
tide is six.

71
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:34,361
Your turn. Find the amplitude, period and
midline of y equals two sine three x minus

72
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:34,665
one.

73
00:05:35,115 --> 00:05:36,651
Pause the video and try it.

74
00:05:37,101 --> 00:05:42,584
Amplitude two, period two pi over three,
midline y equals negative one.

